dsv41 patch 0077 (22.10.2026): KV offload (OffloadingConnector + TieringOffloadingSpec) with TP across two nodes.
In use from image v24 (VLLM_DSV41_OFFLOAD_MULTINODE=1, with --kv-transfer-config and 0081).
Why: each node has its own CPU tier (/dev/shm mmap), and the scheduler's secondary tiers (file tier) only read and write
the head node's copy. vLLM allows the one-copy layout only for single-node TP (nnodes_within_dp == 1), so with two nodes
each rank kept a per-rank slot, both ranks used slot 0 (device index 0 on both nodes), the file tier stored rank 0's
pages plus zeros, and after a load from disk rank 1 read whatever its own CPU slot held last. In production
(22.10. 02:08-11:13) that gave garbage text, NaN logits (xgrammar rejecting token 0) and an illegal memory access on
rank 1. Our first reload test had passed: after a restart rank 1's CPU tier was zero and the summary stayed plausible.
How: every offloaded layer of DSv4.1 holds one latent KV head, the same bytes on every TP rank (checked: both ranks'
CPU tiers byte-identical after the same stores, 256/256 MiB blocks equal). With the switch, config.py takes the
one-copy layout (only rank 0 stores; the file tier is half the size; the CPU tier holds twice the chunks), and before
each load the offloading worker copies the load's CPU chunks from rank 0's region into the other ranks' regions over
a dedicated gloo group (GLOO_SOCKET_IFNAME = fabric NIC); then every rank loads from its own region as before.
VLLM_DSV41_OFFLOAD_MN_VERIFY=1 lets rank 1 store too (for comparing the regions).
Measured (22.10.2026, image v23, 2x GB10, spark2 at 2000 MHz):
  - needle prompts (three codes, first answer token p ~ 1) stored, serve restarted, asked again: 3/3 prefixes of ~75K
    tokens from the file tier in 1.3-2.1 s instead of 33.8 s, codes right, first-token KL 0.0003-0.0035 against a
    fresh compute (two fresh computes: 0.0009-0.0011), two background requests with JSON schema next to it fine;
    the same test with the old layout hung (see 0081) and gave garbage before;
  - copy rank 0 -> rank 1 ~1.7 GB/s (815 chunks, 108 MiB in 65-85 ms); corruption test 0 hits, 0 errors in both ranks'
    logs; decode +0.27 % (geometric mean, worst cell -0.7 %) against v22 measured right after under the same clock.
Switch: VLLM_DSV41_OFFLOAD_MULTINODE=1 (scheduler and workers); default off = vLLM's per-rank layout.
